\end{align*}
Move $19$ to the left-hand side
\begin{align*}
9^x & = \left( \frac{9}{10}\right) ^x - 19\\
9^x & = \frac{9^x}{10^x} - 19\\
\end{align*}

Step 6: Rearranging the terms:
\begin{align*}
9^x- \frac{9^x}{10^x} & =- 19\\
9^x\cdot\left(1-\frac{1}{10^x}\right)& =- 19\\
\end{align*}

Now we want to factor $\frac{9^x}{10^x}$ from the LHS of the equation. 

$$9^x\cdot\left(1-\frac{1}{10^x}\right)=\left(\frac{9\cdot10^x-9}{10^x}\right)$$
 Multiplying numerator and denominator by $10^x$:
\begin{align*}
\left(\frac{9\cdot10^x-9}{10^x}\right) &=\frac{\left(9\cdot10^x\right)-9}{10^x} \\\
&= \left(\frac{9\cdot 10^x}{10^x}\right)- \frac{9}{10^x}\\
&= 9 -\frac{9}{10^x}\\
\end{align*}Substituting it to the equation we had:
\begin{align*}
9 -\frac{9}{10^x} & =- 19\\
\end{align*}
Step 7: To isolate $-\frac{9}{10^x}$ on the left-hand side, we add $19$ to both sides:
\begin{align*}
-\frac{9}{10^x} & = -28 \\
\end{align*}
Multiply both sides by $-1$:
 \begin{align*}
\frac{9}{10^x} & = 28 \\
\end{align*}
We can now multiply both sides by $10^x$ to get rid of the denominator.
 \begin{align*}
9 &=28\cdot10^x\\
\end{align*}
 Divide both sides by 28:
 \begin{align*}
\frac{9}{28}&=10^x
\end{align*}

Now that we have $10^x=\frac{9}{28}$, we can solve for $x$ using the fact that $10^x$ can be expressed as $\log_{10}(\frac{9}{28})$.

Let us solve $10^x=\frac{9}{28}$ for $x$
